p 2 (the arm itself), turn right and go 4, turn right and go 8, turn right and go 12, and so on.\n- **Pair 2 (L = 3):** go up 3, then repeat the unit `[turn right, go a; turn right, go 1; turn left, go 3]` with a = 2, 6, 10, 14…\n  - The short \"turn right, go 1; turn left, go 3\" part is a one-cell jog. It is needed because an arm of length 3 overshoots the radius-2 spiral by one cell.\n- **General formula:** the same unit works with jog length L−2 and final stretch L:\n  `[turn right, go a_k; turn right, go L−2; turn left, go L]`, where a_k = 4k + 2.\n  - For L = 2 the jog is 0 long, so each side is a_k + 2 = 4, 8, 12, which matches pair 3.\n- **Pair 1 (L = 1):** the arm first grows one extra cell to length 2 (up 2 from the centre), then behaves exactly like L = 2 (sides 4, 8, 12…). So I use L′ = max(L, 2), which grows a length-1 arm to length 2.\n\n**Same path for all four arms.** The right, down and left arms follow the same turtle program. They start along their own direction, and the turtle keeps turning clockwise. Line segments are clipped to the grid. The turtle stops once it is far outside.\n\n**Applying it to the test.** The test has centre (12,10) and L = 4. So each arm continues with jogs of length 2 and final stretches of length 4. I checked by hand that the four L = 4 spirals interleave with single-cell gaps and never touch each other.\n\n**Program.** It finds the centre as the mean of the 8-cells and sets L = (number of 8-cells) / 4. It draws each arm's spiral in turn and emits the canvas after each arm is finished.","code":"def transform(grid):\n    g = np.array(grid)\n    H, W = g.shape\n    bg = background(g)\n    cells = np.argwhere(g != bg)\n    col = int(g[cells[0][0], cells[0][1]])\n    cy = int(round(cells[:, 0].mean())); cx = int(round(cells[:, 1].mean()))\n    L = len(cells) // 4\n    Lp = max(L, 2)\n    out = g.copy()\n\n    def cw(d):\n        return (d[1], -d[0])\n\n    def ccw(d):\n        return (-d[1], d[0])\n\n    lim = 2 * (H + W) + 10\n    for d0 in [(-1, 0), (0, 1), (1, 0), (0, -1)]:\n        pos = [cy, cx]\n        d = d0\n\n        def walk(n, d):\n            for _ in range(n):\n                pos[0] += d[0]; pos[1] += d[1]\n                if 0 <= pos[0] < H and 0 <= pos[1] < W:\n                    out[pos[0], pos[1]] = col\n\n        walk(Lp, d)\n        k = 0\n        while True:\n            a = 4 * k + 2\n            if a > lim:\n                break\n            d = cw(d); walk(a, d)\n            d = cw(d); walk(Lp - 2, d)\n            d = ccw(d); walk(Lp, d)\n            k += 1\n        emit(out, f\"Arm starting in direction {d0}: extended (to length {Lp}) and wound clockwise as a spiral with jogs of {Lp-2} and stretches of {Lp}.\")\n    emit(out, \"All four spirals drawn; final grid.\")\n    return out\n","candidate_tactics":{"paths":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"Every corridor between the lines is exactly one cell wide."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"**What each arm does.** I traced the path of the up arm in each demonstration."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"Here the path is written as segment lengths and turns."}],"rays":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"It is needed because an arm of length 3 overshoots the radius-2 spiral by one cell."}],"count":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"**Program.** It finds the centre as the mean of the 8-cells and sets L = (number of 8-cells) / 4."}],"instructions":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"**Same path for all four arms.** The right, down and left arms follow the same turtle program."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"They start along their own direction, and the turtle keeps turning clockwise."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"The turtle stops once it is far outside."}]},"provenance":{"reference_assisted":false,"query_labels_used_for_rule_diagnosis":false},"artifact_sha256":"154f99d8f4cd4fc72589b87d92ad9cf8837a04e41b6b499b0f43c1a60f1fa54c","task_sha256":"f254db07a48f161b6548f131a520cf54